              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Sarah Jo"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this recipe exact, only leaving out the olive because I don't care for them. One of the best 'tini recipes I've ever had."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "robertdeblois"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good, I had Cointreau and triple sec, but not enough of either one for a large batch (made for 16), so I did 1/2 and 1/2 and used crystal light for the \"orange juice\"...came out fabulous!! Garnished with a line wedge vs an olive... Guests loved it!!",
            "datePublished": "2016-12-02T01:41:11.203Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mikaela Cox"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Deeeelicious! I used my blender to turn the ice into snow, so it turned into a type of slushie once the mix was added. I used Triple Sec and will be trying it with Grand Marnier next time! Yum!",
            "datePublished": "2019-02-02T19:01:59.793Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "karinagw"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I realize that there is, technically, a difference between the various orange liqueurs, but I tend to use them interchangeably depending on what I have on hand and what I usually have on hand is blue curacao (exactly the same as orange curacao except the color)because I love the effect it gives the beverage. As another person commented, I too was out of S&amp;S mix and used Margarita mix. Still really good, but I think it might benefit from actual S&amp;S mix (or maybe a lime/simple syrup combo). Will just have to force myself to keep on making it until I get it perfected.",
            "datePublished": "2014-08-18T16:51:29.663Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "bellacucina"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These were really good and easy to make. I did not have sweet and sour mixer so I just used JC margarita mix and they still tasted great. I kept the ice in my glass and added a straw.",
            "datePublished": "2010-07-20T12:43:58.590Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "The Bunny Chef"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is quite good, but the sweet and sour mix is pretty strong and makes it very sweet. I suggest swapping the amounts of orange juice and sweet and sour mix for maximum deliciousness :) Will definitely make again!",
            "datePublished": "2012-03-18T04:46:46.417Z"
          },
          {
